*private
*.private
*.private.*
# AI assistant settings and state. Never sync these: they carry
# permission rules, MCP server configs, per-project transcripts and
# memory. $HOME/.claude/statusline.sh is the one deliberate exception.
#
# `**/` on each: a pattern holding a slash anchors to this file's own directory,
# so `$HOME/.claude/settings.json` looks for one directly beside .gitignore and
# matches nothing under root/. These read as covered while covering nothing, and
# what actually ignores them on a configured machine is the operator's global
# ignore, which a fresh clone does not have.
**/.claude/projects/
**/.claude/CLAUDE.md
**/.claude/settings.json
**/.claude/settings.local.json
**/.claude.json
**/.config/claude-code/
**/.gemini/
**/.antigravity/
**/.config/Antigravity/
**/.cursor/
**/.continue/
**/.config/opencode/
**/.config/kilo/
# Unanchored, so one written anywhere in the tree is caught and not only the
# copy under root/$HOME. A session working from a fresh clone has no global
# ignore to fall back on.
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
GEMINI.md
# Editor state, which carries the text of a file and not only its name. `sops
# edit` decrypts into a tempfile and the editor writes an undo history for it,
# so a synced undo directory takes plaintext out of the store and into a public
# repository. The directory rather than the sops names: an undo file is named
# after the path it belongs to, and every file edited has one.
#
# `**/` because a pattern holding a slash anchors to this file's own directory,
# and the tree it has to match is under root/.
**/.vim/tmp/
# Unanchored for the reason the three above are: a sops file is named for what
# it holds wherever it lands, including a copy made outside root/$HOME.
*.sops.yml
*.sops.yaml
*.sops.json
